---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('attentive')!;
const b = appBySlug('mailchimp')!;
const path = '/compare/attentive-vs-mailchimp';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/attentive-vs-mailchimp');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/attentive-vs-mailchimp');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can Mailchimp remain the email owner?",
    "answer": "Yes, if the connected Shopify events, eligible audience and purchase exits meet the store\u2019s requirements and the SMS handoff is demonstrated."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Transactional replace recovery marketing?",
    "answer": "No automatic substitution is established. Mailchimp Transactional is a separate API/SMTP product; a promotional cart reminder needs its own audience and purpose assessment."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is Attentive email automatically enabled?",
    "answer": "Attentive documents triggered email journeys but instructs merchants to request access; confirm enablement in the selected account."
  },
  {
    "question": "Do these examples prove a winner?",
    "answer": "No. The workflows are proposed acceptance exercises. Compare deduplicated contribution and operator effort with uncertainty before choosing."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-16"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Checkout event ownership and the transactional boundary: compare permissions, event handoffs and operating cost with your own checkout.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{label:"Attentive",tool:a.name,reason:"Evaluate email and SMS journeys after confirming channel access."},{label:"Mailchimp",tool:b.name,reason:"Yes, if the connected Shopify events, eligible audience and purchase exits meet the store\u2019s requirements and the SMS handoff is demonstrated."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Attentive</th><th>Mailchimp</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Operating focus</td><td>Email access and channel ownership</td><td>Checkout event ownership and the transactional boundary</td></tr>
<tr><td>Required evidence</td><td>Enabled journey, permission and purchase exit</td><td>Keep marketing recovery separate from Transactional</td></tr>
<tr><td>Budget</td><td>Current channel and service quote</td><td>Current scope plus handoff and staff costs</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Compare account continuity with a combined journey</h2>
<p>Attentive supports triggered email in cart and Shopify checkout abandonment journeys. Its <a href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/4408727834772-Emails-in-journeys">email journey guide</a> instructs merchants to request access. Evaluate it when combining email and SMS under that operating setup would reduce handoffs; verify actual access and purchase exits before replacing an existing email workflow.</p>
<h2>Inspect the Shopify event in Mailchimp</h2>
<p><a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/connect-shopify/">Mailchimp’s Shopify integration guide</a> describes syncing customer and purchase data for marketing. Use a test shopper to establish what your connected account receives at cart creation, checkout start and purchase. Save payloads and timestamps, including product variants and the destination link. A connected store does not prove that every anonymous browser can enter recovery.</p>
<h2>Keep marketing recovery separate from Transactional</h2>
<p><a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/about-transactional-email/">Mailchimp distinguishes Marketing from Transactional</a>, its separate API/SMTP sending product. Do not classify a promotional recovery message as a receipt just because checkout triggered it. Have the marketing owner establish the permitted audience and purpose; keep genuine order confirmations and their application sender in the service workflow. Transactional access does not by itself demonstrate Shopify marketing automation.</p>
<h2>Use the shipping question to test the service boundary</h2>
<p>A shopper may ask about international delivery after starting checkout. Support should answer from the current shipping quote and record when the issue is resolved. A promotional discount requires the recovery owner’s approval; the service reply should not silently enroll the shopper in SMS. Repeat the exercise for gift sizing and a financing question, where human clarification may be more useful than urgency.</p>
<h2>Prove exits across an existing email and SMS stack</h2>
<p>If Mailchimp remains the email owner and Attentive sends SMS, inventory Shopify’s own recovery messages too. Purchase between scheduled steps, opt out of one channel, and replay the checkout event. Check each queue using customer, cart and order identifiers. Hold the affected branch if a sender cannot obtain current purchase or permission state. Email permission is not SMS permission, and a reply is not permission for an unrelated campaign.</p>
<h2>Cost continuity before deciding to migrate</h2>
<p>Normalize contact count, email volume, SMS destinations, automation access and any separately required Transactional usage. Include template migration, event mapping and staff time. Keeping an established Mailchimp account can avoid rebuilding working campaigns, but that advantage must be weighed against maintaining a second queue. No evidence here supports a claim that lifecycle stores inevitably migrate within a year.</p>
<h2>Keep incentive economics separate from attribution</h2>
<p>Illustrative $245 basket at 38% gross margin: goods cost $151.90, full-price gross profit $93.10. A 20% order-wide code leaves $196.00 revenue and $44.10 gross profit, a $49.00 reduction with unchanged goods cost. Subtract fulfillment, payment, message and return costs before calling the remainder contribution. This arithmetic is not an observed recovery result or a recommended discount cap.</p>
<h2>Cut over with a measurable decision</h2>
<p>Pause competing recovery queues before enabling their replacement. Reconcile purchase, refund, stock and permission state, save a rollback owner and inspect the return link. Choose timing from checkout behavior and local sending constraints. For a feasible randomized no-message comparison, select allocation and duration from traffic and conversion delay. Deduplicate orders across senders and compare contribution per eligible shopper; a platform-attributed order alone does not establish incremental lift.</p>
<p>Primary product scope checked November 25, 2026. The linked documentation establishes the stated scope; the operational exercises above remain proposals for your team.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section></BaseLayout>
